I did an install yesterday on a PC164LX, when you go from the SRM console to
boot to the CDROM
give the command
boot <yourscsicdrom name> ( via show dev on srm console)

once aboot loads it shows you a list of commands (or via ? on srm console
anytime)
l (lower case L) should list you the available kernels with their boot paths
etc.

Its possible you partitioned the disk incorrectly and have not left space at
the beginning, make sure your first partition starts AFTER
a few cylinders (sya 10 or so) this will allow the boot block to install
References :http://www.linuxalpha.org/faq/aboot.html

Below is o/p of what I told you

>>> boot dka0 -fl i
aboot> ?
 h, ?                   Display this message
 q                      Halt the system and return to SRM
 p 1-8                  Look in partition <num> for configuration/kernel
 l                      List pre-configured kernels
 d <dir>                List directory <dir> in current filesystem
 b <file> <args>        Boot kernel in <file> (- for raw boot)
                        with arguments <args>
 0-9                    Boot pre-configuration 0-9 (list with 'l')


C.

