Not sure if these apply to your situation (x86 specific)

In x86 linux you have to tell the default ide kernel module not to "see" the 
/dev/hd<a-d> the cdrw is on and instead pass the option "/dev/hda=ide-scsi" 
to the kernel at bootup (since ide-scsi cannot find the cdrw if it is 
already in control of the default ide driver)

there are more (x86 specific) details in the CD Writing Howto 
http://www.tldp.org/HOWTO/CD-Writing-HOWTO.html

