It wrote a reasonable aboot.conf when I was installing that distro
but maybe there are some bugs.  In such case for this 7.2 Alpha they
should be reported to

  http://alpha.crl.dec.com/bugzilla

and not in "usual" places.

But even if aboot.conf is messed up then you can list a content of you
boot directory from 'aboot> ' prompt and type explicitely the whole
boot command (whatever is in aboot.conf can be listed with 'l' and used
as examples).  So "couldn't boot" really should read "is somewhat
inconvenient to type the whole boot command explicitely until aboot.conf
can be edited".

> BTW, your boot command:
> 
>>boot sdb5:/boot/vmlinuz root=/dev/sdb5
> 
> doesn't look right, the name of the kernel looks incomplete:

'aboot' understands links so if 'vmlinuz' was made into a link to
a right kernel this may work.  OTOH if you will update a kernel then
this link may become invalid and you will need to boot explicitely
again to get an opportunity to straigten that up.

If you attempt to mount your '/' as ext3 file system then initrd is
also needed as in distribution kernels a support for that is loaded
as a module.

   Michal


